{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0 Calibri;}}\f0\fs24 Reading a Brassica Catalogue: How to Avoid Duplicate Cabbage and Kale Lines \u183? Questions and answers\par Brassica catalogues often contain lines that look distinct in print but overlap when stock is reviewed, especially where cabbage, kale and leafy greens share genus names and horticultural group labels. This question-and-answer guide sets out a practical routine for reading such a catalogue, so that staff can spot related records before treating one as a new line. It is based on how the wholesale cabbage and kale listing and the mixed leaf vegetable listing present their entries.\par \b Why do duplicates appear in a Brassica catalogue in the first place?\b0\par Duplicates rarely come from the species level. They come from the qualifier. A broad botanical label can make two different leaf selections look like a single line, or make an existing selection look like a new addition when it is already listed under different wording. Because some records use horticultural group names instead of one accepted species name, the printed variety name alone is not a reliable basis for deciding whether a line is already on the shelf.\par \b Where should a duplicate check begin?\b0\par Start with the species record, not the variety name. A shared genus does not make names interchangeable, and two references under the same broad heading may still describe different material. Confirm that both entries sit under the same species or convar. before comparing anything else; only then move on to the qualifier. Where the imported botanical label is broad, the catalogue advises requesting clarification against the SKU rather than guessing from the printed name.\par \b Which layers of naming need to be read separately?\b0\par A Brassica entry exposes several layers, and reading them in order prevents near-identical entries from being stocked twice. First, the broad species or group label, such as Brassica oleracea convar. acephala, which covers kale-type material. Second, the horticultural group column, where Brassica greens, Kale and Kale (curly kale) each appear as separate groups. Third, the leaf-type and cultivar qualifier, which distinguishes one reference from another within a group. Curly kale, for example, is listed as its own labelled group with two varieties, while plain kale sits in a related group with three varieties; these are related records, not duplicate ones.\par \b What is a short comparison routine for staff?\b0\par A four-step check keeps both the shelf and the records clean. Match the species first. Compare the group column next, since Brassica greens, kale and curly kale remain separate groups even when they share a genus. Then read the full qualifier aloud, because leaf-type and cultivar text is part of the reference, not decoration. Finally, check the SKU; if the label remains ambiguous, the SKU resolves it. This routine also protects customer-facing descriptions, since a shortened shelf-card name may no longer be clear to the buyer or to the person reordering.\par \b Do leafy greens belong in the same comparison?\b0\par Not automatically. Some Brassica material does not sit under cabbage and kale at all. The leaf vegetable listing is a mixed botanical group covering Beta, Brassica, Cichorium and Spinacia names, and it includes separate Brassica records such as Brassica juncea mustard greens and Brassica rapa ssp. pekinensis napa cabbage. The catalogue is explicit that these are not interchangeable simply because both sit under leafy greens: a napa cabbage record is not the same line as a curly kale record, and neither should be duplicated as a stocking shortcut.\par \b How should a resale enquiry be framed once duplicates are ruled out?\b0\par Identify the SKU and the required weight. A price per kilogram is a different basis from a price for a stated number of seeds, so compare only within the product's recorded unit. Pack size, available quantity and final price are checked in the quotation. A gap in a Brassica range is not automatically filled by a new line; in many cases the reference already exists under a group label or a qualified botanical name, so read the species, then the group, then the qualifier before deciding whether the shelf needs anything added.\par {\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "https://info0277593.wixsite.com/bulk-seeds-internati/beitrag/reading-brassica-catalogue-avoid-duplicate-lines"}{\fldrslt Reading a Brassica Catalogue: How to Avoid Duplicate Cabbage and Kale Lines}}\par {\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "https://bulk-seeds-international.github.io/bulkseedsinternational-en/dateien/minimax-6ff54ddf251d40c4e639a1f5/inhalt.odt"}{\fldrslt Related document}}\par }